1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:02,550
In this video, we'll expand on

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:04,620
the signal concepts that we

3
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:06,330
talked about in the
previous video and talk

4
00:00:06,330 --> 00:00:08,845
more about how to
manage signals.

5
00:00:08,845 --> 00:00:11,490
We're going to learn how
to write signal handlers,

6
00:00:11,490 --> 00:00:13,560
and we're going to talk
about the details of writing

7
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,290
a signal handler and
dealing with reentrancy.

8
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:20,520
The book talks about
the signal function

9
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,665
as one way to set
up signal handlers.

10
00:00:22,665 --> 00:00:25,079
But if you look at the
latest documentation

11
00:00:25,079 --> 00:00:27,285
from the Linux
programmer's manual,

12
00:00:27,285 --> 00:00:31,365
it recommends not
using signal anymore

13
00:00:31,365 --> 00:00:35,610
due to some variation across
older versions of Linux,

14
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:38,085
and it suggests using
sigaction instead.

15
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:41,940
That's what we'll go
over in this video.

16
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:47,260
Sigaction is a POSIX alternative
to the signal function.

17
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:50,770
It wasn't a part of the
original standard C library,

18
00:00:50,770 --> 00:00:54,830
but it does have better signal
management capabilities.

19
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,960
Those better signal management
capabilities include

20
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:01,510
the ability to block new
signals during the handler,

21
00:01:01,510 --> 00:01:04,495
because as we mentioned
in the previous video,

22
00:01:04,495 --> 00:01:07,610
signals could interrupt
other signals.

23
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:11,305
That's probably not the
behavior that you want.

24
00:01:11,305 --> 00:01:13,420
Sigaction gives you an option

25
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:15,740
to prevent that from occurring.

26
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,990
The better single
management capabilities

27
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:21,250
also include retrieving

28
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:22,750
state information
about the system.

29
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:24,685
There's some additional
information you can

30
00:01:24,685 --> 00:01:27,770
retrieve as a part
of the handler.

31
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:31,250
It looks a little bit cumbersome
to use here in terms of

32
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:33,560
all the different
sigaction content

33
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:34,610
that you can set up here in

34
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:36,115
the structure that
you're passing.

35
00:01:36,115 --> 00:01:37,880
However, for the simplest cases,

36
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,520
you really only need to set
up this signal handler.

37
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:42,545
It's very similar to

38
00:01:42,545 --> 00:01:44,630
the inner action that

39
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:47,185
you need to have with
the signal function.

40
00:01:47,185 --> 00:01:49,925
Once you register for a signal,

41
00:01:49,925 --> 00:01:51,950
you can use pause to wait for

42
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:53,195
a signal if that's

43
00:01:53,195 --> 00:01:54,930
a part of what your
program needs to do.

44
00:01:54,930 --> 00:01:57,080
In some cases, your program
might just register for

45
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,285
signals and then wait
for those to happen.

46
00:01:59,285 --> 00:02:02,100
In that case, you'd
use pause to do that.

47
00:02:02,510 --> 00:02:06,275
You can use kill to send
a signal to a process.

48
00:02:06,275 --> 00:02:07,970
It's a little confusing,

49
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:11,615
because you might think it
would be sending SIGKILL.

50
00:02:11,615 --> 00:02:13,520
That's what I would
do by default.

51
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:16,100
But you can really send
any signal to it with

52
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,395
either the command line option

53
00:02:18,395 --> 00:02:21,135
or the argument that
you're passing.

54
00:02:21,135 --> 00:02:24,200
It's available either as a
system call or a user command,

55
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:25,610
meaning I can type kill from

56
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:27,500
the terminal and invoke kill on

57
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,660
a process or send

58
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:33,055
a signal that I
specified to a process,

59
00:02:33,055 --> 00:02:37,740
or I can call it from
within my own program.

60
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:41,465
Here's a really simple example

61
00:02:41,465 --> 00:02:43,775
of a sigaction signal handling,

62
00:02:43,775 --> 00:02:45,830
where we register here with

63
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:49,055
sigaction for either
SIGTERM or SIGINT.

64
00:02:49,055 --> 00:02:53,015
We set up the new action
signal handler here,

65
00:02:53,015 --> 00:02:54,170
which is pointing to

66
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:56,030
our signal handler function

67
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:57,980
that we've defined right here.

68
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:59,810
When we get a signal,

69
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:02,120
we'll get a call to signal

70
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:03,275
handler that's going to

71
00:03:03,275 --> 00:03:06,115
include the signal
number that we received.

72
00:03:06,115 --> 00:03:08,720
We're using the
pause function here.

73
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:12,300
If we were successful in
setting up both of the signals,

74
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:14,780
we'll use pause to
wait for a signal,

75
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:17,090
and then we'll just
log what we received.

76
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:18,680
The way we're communicating this

77
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,480
is just through a global
variable that we're

78
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:23,150
setting in the signal
handler and we're

79
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:26,490
reading outside of
the signal handler.

80
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:29,700
The fact that we're only setting

81
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:32,030
this value in the signal
handler and we're

82
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:34,490
only reading it outside
the signal handler,

83
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:36,785
is part of the reason that
we won't have to worry about

84
00:03:36,785 --> 00:03:40,110
the reentrancy that
we'll talk about later.

85
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:43,190
An overview of how you'd use

86
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:45,200
this really simple function,

87
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,640
and you can find the source here

88
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,880
if you want to try it yourself.

89
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,870
You can invoke with

90
00:03:50,870 --> 00:03:53,660
the signal handler executable

91
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:55,040
and you'll see a
message that says,

92
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:56,495
waiting forever for a signal.

93
00:03:56,495 --> 00:03:58,370
If you use Control-C
at that point,

94
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:00,445
you'll be able to send SIGINT.

95
00:04:00,445 --> 00:04:02,230
Once you send a SIGINT,

96
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:05,060
you should see the message to
say that you caught SIGINT.

97
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:07,855
A second way you can use this

98
00:04:07,855 --> 00:04:11,895
is instead of sending
via Control-C,

99
00:04:11,895 --> 00:04:13,965
you could send term
through the kill command.

100
00:04:13,965 --> 00:04:15,665
In this case, you could restart

101
00:04:15,665 --> 00:04:17,540
and then open a new
terminal window,

102
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,505
use PS to find the process.

103
00:04:19,505 --> 00:04:22,405
In this case, it was 31236.

104
00:04:22,405 --> 00:04:25,245
Then I can say kill-term 31236.

105
00:04:25,245 --> 00:04:26,789
That's going to send SIGTERM

106
00:04:26,789 --> 00:04:29,025
to the process and you'll
see the same result.

107
00:04:29,025 --> 00:04:30,630
You'll get that you caught

108
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:34,540
the SIGTERM and the
process will exit.

109
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:38,990
Siqueue is a way that

110
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:41,345
you could actually send
a payload to a signal.

111
00:04:41,345 --> 00:04:44,840
The arguments here will
allow you to provide

112
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:46,715
a function pointer that would

113
00:04:46,715 --> 00:04:49,610
provide additional information
to the signal handler.

114
00:04:49,610 --> 00:04:52,790
This can be used as a
rudimentary form of IPC,

115
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:55,190
although it's probably not
the best way to do IPC,

116
00:04:55,190 --> 00:04:57,640
which we'll talk about
later in the video.

117
00:04:57,640 --> 00:04:59,830
Some additional signal concepts,

118
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:03,720
we've talked about forking a
process in previous videos.

119
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:07,130
When you fork a process and
you create a child process,

120
00:05:07,130 --> 00:05:08,270
the child is going to inherit

121
00:05:08,270 --> 00:05:10,340
the signal actions of
the parent on the fork.

122
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:11,645
Whatever the parents set up,

123
00:05:11,645 --> 00:05:16,985
if the parent thread setup
custom signal actions,

124
00:05:16,985 --> 00:05:19,445
those will be inherited
by the child on fork.

125
00:05:19,445 --> 00:05:22,730
If you then would start
a process with exec,

126
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:25,070
which remember is
part of the sequence

127
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:26,270
for starting a daemon that

128
00:05:26,270 --> 00:05:28,070
we discussed in
the previous week.

129
00:05:28,070 --> 00:05:30,470
The exec call is

130
00:05:30,470 --> 00:05:32,825
going to reset all signals
to the default actions,

131
00:05:32,825 --> 00:05:34,640
and that's the case for
everything other than

132
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:38,190
the signals that were previously
ignored by the parent.

133
00:05:38,950 --> 00:05:42,500
A little bit about
writing signal handlers

134
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:45,650
and safety inside signal
handler functions.

135
00:05:45,650 --> 00:05:48,740
Because signal handlers are
going to suspend execution of

136
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:50,330
a process and then
just jump into

137
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:52,760
the signal handler function,

138
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:54,425
it's important to
know this is not

139
00:05:54,425 --> 00:05:59,150
a thread switch to a different
thread or a new thread.

140
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,160
It's going to reuse
the existing thread,

141
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:03,290
whatever was running,
execution is going

142
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:05,540
to stop and it's going to
switch to our signal handlers.

143
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,250
That has some important
implications on

144
00:06:07,250 --> 00:06:09,705
how we can write
signal handlers.

145
00:06:09,705 --> 00:06:12,235
What does this mean about
the functions you can call?

146
00:06:12,235 --> 00:06:14,815
It means they must
be async-signal-safe

147
00:06:14,815 --> 00:06:18,345
in the Single Unix
Specification parlance.

148
00:06:18,345 --> 00:06:22,430
It means that they must be
reentrant and also they must

149
00:06:22,430 --> 00:06:24,260
block signals at any point

150
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:26,000
that could cause
inconsistencies.

151
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,464
Because even if your
reentrant function,

152
00:06:29,464 --> 00:06:31,280
if it's a possible
that you could take

153
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:34,700
a second signal handler
or a second signal,

154
00:06:34,700 --> 00:06:37,100
you need to make sure that
that signal handler could also

155
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:40,790
execute without causing
inconsistencies.

156
00:06:40,790 --> 00:06:43,430
How do you know if a
POSIX function that

157
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:45,470
you're calling is signal safe?

158
00:06:45,470 --> 00:06:47,120
Really the only way to know is

159
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:50,210
to look at the table
of allowed functions.

160
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:52,010
There's one that's in
your book and there's

161
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:53,120
a more complete one that I've

162
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:55,250
linked in the slide below here.

163
00:06:55,250 --> 00:06:58,894
But the main takeaway
is that malloc, free,

164
00:06:58,894 --> 00:07:01,805
any functions that might
call malloc and free

165
00:07:01,805 --> 00:07:06,410
and just general stdio
library functions,

166
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:08,570
so for instance,
printf are generally

167
00:07:08,570 --> 00:07:12,690
not really safe to call
from within a signal.

168
00:07:13,110 --> 00:07:16,075
The signal function
that you write

169
00:07:16,075 --> 00:07:18,720
or any functions that you
call from a signal handler,

170
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:20,420
must not be manipulating

171
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:21,800
static data that could be used

172
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:23,270
outside the signal handler.

173
00:07:23,270 --> 00:07:24,680
There's a handful of these that

174
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:26,150
are a part of the POSIX spec,

175
00:07:26,150 --> 00:07:29,390
str signal is one example
that the book gives.

176
00:07:29,390 --> 00:07:33,260
But anything that's returning
a pointer to, for instance,

177
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:36,245
memory that's used in some
kind of static structure,

178
00:07:36,245 --> 00:07:39,545
that won't be safe to use
inside of signal handler.

179
00:07:39,545 --> 00:07:43,310
Ideally, your signal
handler would only

180
00:07:43,310 --> 00:07:44,990
stack allocated data or

181
00:07:44,990 --> 00:07:47,630
data that's provided
by the caller.

182
00:07:47,630 --> 00:07:50,690
If you want to make a really
simple signal handler,

183
00:07:50,690 --> 00:07:52,070
often the easiest
way to do this is

184
00:07:52,070 --> 00:07:53,825
just to set a global flag.

185
00:07:53,825 --> 00:07:55,790
If you just set in
the signal handler

186
00:07:55,790 --> 00:07:57,800
and just read outside
the signal handler,

187
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:00,845
that's one safe way that
you can do reentrancy.

188
00:08:00,845 --> 00:08:03,050
But it's important that
you must not invoke

189
00:08:03,050 --> 00:08:05,720
any non-async-signal-
safe function

190
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:07,950
from within your signal handler.

191
00:08:08,230 --> 00:08:10,520
The best practices
if you're going to

192
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:12,005
write a signal handler routine,

193
00:08:12,005 --> 00:08:14,690
you want to minimize
global data access and

194
00:08:14,690 --> 00:08:16,670
any global data access
that you do need to

195
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:18,995
confirm that it's
async-signal-safe.

196
00:08:18,995 --> 00:08:20,900
In other words, if at

197
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:25,685
any process in your
existing program,

198
00:08:25,685 --> 00:08:27,814
if you were accessing
that variable,

199
00:08:27,814 --> 00:08:29,120
it could be interrupted,

200
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:30,530
take the signal handler and it

201
00:08:30,530 --> 00:08:33,870
won't impact the way
the program works.

202
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:37,955
We talked about how errno
know is stored per thread.

203
00:08:37,955 --> 00:08:40,700
However, because we're
interrupting the current thread,

204
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:42,470
if you call a function
that modifies

205
00:08:42,470 --> 00:08:44,300
errno inside your
signal handler,

206
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:46,235
you are going to change
the value of errno,

207
00:08:46,235 --> 00:08:47,900
so to be safe you should

208
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:50,855
save and restore errno
in your signal handler.

209
00:08:50,855 --> 00:08:54,365
Then call an absolute
minimum set of functions

210
00:08:54,365 --> 00:08:55,700
and only the
functions that are on

211
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:57,725
approved list of signal
handler functions.

212
00:08:57,725 --> 00:08:59,210
You really want to make
your signal handler

213
00:08:59,210 --> 00:09:00,230
be as simple as

214
00:09:00,230 --> 00:09:02,030
possible and be really diligent

215
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:04,880
about what functions
you call within it.

216
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:07,250
Why would we want to use,

217
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:08,810
or why would we not
want to use signals?

218
00:09:08,810 --> 00:09:10,745
Well, after the discussion
that we just had

219
00:09:10,745 --> 00:09:12,665
about all the things you need to

220
00:09:12,665 --> 00:09:14,615
make sure of in terms
of signal handlers,

221
00:09:14,615 --> 00:09:16,520
you can see that we're
pretty restricted

222
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:18,710
about what we can do
in a signal handler.

223
00:09:18,710 --> 00:09:20,870
One of the biggest
reasons not to use

224
00:09:20,870 --> 00:09:23,015
signals would be that there's

225
00:09:23,015 --> 00:09:24,200
significant risk in

226
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:27,800
the signal handler
safety and reentrancy,

227
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:29,855
and if you mess that up,

228
00:09:29,855 --> 00:09:31,250
those problems can be easy to

229
00:09:31,250 --> 00:09:33,380
introduce and really
difficult to track down.

230
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:35,090
You really have to
be careful about

231
00:09:35,090 --> 00:09:36,590
implementing your handlers and

232
00:09:36,590 --> 00:09:38,060
they're really limited
on what you can

233
00:09:38,060 --> 00:09:39,845
do from a signal handler,

234
00:09:39,845 --> 00:09:42,050
so that's one reason you
might not want to use it.

235
00:09:42,050 --> 00:09:45,185
The book references signals as

236
00:09:45,185 --> 00:09:46,700
old antiquated mechanism for

237
00:09:46,700 --> 00:09:48,200
kernel to user communication,

238
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:51,260
so they're there for
a specific purpose,

239
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:53,855
they're there for historical
reasons in a lot of cases,

240
00:09:53,855 --> 00:09:55,475
we need to use them

241
00:09:55,475 --> 00:09:57,800
for many important notifications

242
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:58,580
that come from the kernel.

243
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:01,175
Probably the most
important is termination.

244
00:10:01,175 --> 00:10:02,615
It's important to
know about them,

245
00:10:02,615 --> 00:10:03,815
but they're probably
not the best

246
00:10:03,815 --> 00:10:06,570
IPC mechanism that you can use.